{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Usability Testing and Learning Evidence", "title": "Participant Profiles and Sampling", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting participant profiles and sampling to moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence", "ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "02", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/05/01"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_calibration"]}, "content": "# Participant Profiles and Sampling\n\n![Participant Profiles and Sampling textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/05-02-participant-profiles-and-sampling.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA usability finding is only as trustworthy as the people who produced it. You can run a flawless session, log every behavior, and still draw a false conclusion if the participants do not stand in for the learners the design is actually for. This is the validity problem at the heart of sampling: who you recruit silently determines which problems can even appear. Recruit experts and the friction novices feel never surfaces; recruit only the eager and you miss the reluctant learner the course most needs to reach. This section treats the participant profile as a design decision with consequences, not as a logistics chore solved by \"whoever is available.\"\n\nIn AIL 606 the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace must say not just what you tested but who you tested it on, and why those people make a found problem likely to transfer to the real audience. A clean session with the wrong sample is worse than a messy one with the right sample, because it overclaims with confidence.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Define a target population by the trait that drives the design's usability risk, not by convenience.\n- Build a screener that recruits for that trait and excludes participants who would mask the problem.\n- Use small-sample logic to choose a defensible number of participants per relevant subgroup.\n- Span the range of learner variability so a finding is not an artifact of one narrow cluster.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer needs feedback on a self-paced course built for warehouse staff with little prior experience using a learning management system. The fastest people to recruit are five colleagues from the design team. They are sharp, available, and fluent in e-learning, so the session runs smoothly and everyone completes every task. The plan looks validated.\n\nIt is not. The colleagues never stumbled where warehouse staff will stumble, because expertise erased exactly the friction the test was meant to find. The smooth session is not evidence that the design works; it is evidence that the wrong people tried it. The sample, chosen for availability, guaranteed a false positive before anyone clicked a button.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nSampling in formative usability is purposive, not statistical. You are not trying to estimate a population parameter; you are trying to surface design problems, and the math that governs that is the discovery curve. With problems that affect a reasonable share of users, a handful of well-chosen participants exposes most of them, while the next handful mostly repeats what you already saw. The classic rule of thumb (roughly five users per homogeneous group surfaces the bulk of that group's problems) is a budgeting heuristic, not a guarantee, and it collapses the moment your users are not homogeneous.\n\nThat caveat is where **UDL learner variability** does real work. If your audience spans novices and frequent users, or differs on a trait that changes how people interact (language, assistive technology, prior schema), then \"five users\" is five users per meaningfully different group, not five users total. Recruit for the trait that drives risk:\n\n- **Risk-driving trait first.** Screen on the variable most likely to cause failure (prior LMS use, reading level, device), because that is the axis along which problems cluster.\n- **Exclude the maskers.** Participants whose expertise or insider knowledge would hide the problem are not \"extra data\"; they are contamination.\n- **Span the range.** Cover the edges of the variability, not just the comfortable middle, since edge learners reveal problems the median never hits.\n\n**Cognitive load theory** explains why the wrong sample misleads: experts have schemas that absorb extraneous load invisibly, so a screen that overloads a novice feels effortless to them. The expert's success is not transferable evidence.\n\n## Worked Example: Revising a Participant Profile and Sampling Plan\n\nA designer testing a corporate-compliance e-learning prototype drafts a recruitment plan.\n\n**Before (convenience recruitment):**\n\n> Plan: Recruit 5 colleagues from our design team who are available this week. They know e-learning, so they will give good feedback.\n\nWalk through why this sample threatens the evidence, step by step:\n\n1. **Name the target population.** The course is for warehouse staff with low prior LMS experience, not designers.\n2. **Check representativeness.** Design-team colleagues are expert users; they will not stumble where the real population stumbles, so usability problems stay hidden.\n3. **Define a screening criterion.** Recruit by the trait that drives the design risk: prior LMS experience, not availability.\n4. **Set a sample that covers known variability.** Cover the range of the real population (a **UDL learner variability** move), not a single convenient cluster.\n\n**After (criterion-based, varied sample):**\n\n> Plan: Recruit 6 warehouse staff screened by prior LMS use: 2 first-time users, 2 occasional users, 2 frequent users. None work in design or training. Screener question: \"In the past year, how many online courses have you completed at work?\"\n\nThe after version is defensible because the sample is chosen for the trait that drives usability risk and deliberately spans learner variability, so a problem found in the session plausibly transfers to the real audience. The before version would have produced a clean session that overclaims, because experts hid the real friction.\n\nThe four moves above are not interchangeable; they have a logical order, because you cannot screen for a trait before you have named it, and you cannot size the sample before you know which subgroups must appear. Put the recruitment-design steps in the order a defensible plan follows.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the target population by the real audience, not by who is available\", \"Identify the single trait that drives the usability risk\", \"Write a screener question that recruits for that trait and excludes maskers\", \"Set per-subgroup counts that span the relevant variability\", \"Let availability decide only among people who already pass the screener\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRecruit for the trait that breaks the design, then cover its range; let availability decide only among people who already pass the screener. If you find yourself justifying a participant by \"they were free\" rather than \"they match the risk profile,\" stop and rewrite the screener.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your warehouse-staff course needs testers. A design colleague who is fluent in every LMS volunteers and is free today. What does adding them to the sample do?\" explanation=\"Their expertise absorbs the extraneous load that overwhelms novice warehouse staff, so they will glide past the exact friction the test exists to find. They are not extra data, they are contamination that masks the problem and manufactures a clean, overclaiming session.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Adds useful expert benchmarking data\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Masks the novice friction the test is meant to surface, contaminating the result\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Improves coverage of learner variability\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Has no effect as long as the other six testers are warehouse staff\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nBefore locking the sample into your moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, check three things:\n\n1. **Representativeness check:** Does each participant share the trait that drives the usability risk?\n2. **Coverage check:** Do your participants span the variability that matters, or cluster in one comfortable subgroup?\n3. **Contamination check:** Would any participant's expertise hide a problem the real audience would hit?\n\nIf any check fails, the session may run smoothly and still produce evidence that does not transfer.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe frequent error is reading \"five users is enough\" as a universal license, when it holds only within a single homogeneous group; an audience with three meaningfully different segments needs participants in each. A second error is equating a diverse-looking roster with a valid sample: diversity on traits that do not affect the interaction (favorite color, department) adds nothing, while a missing axis that does affect it (no screen-reader users for an accessibility claim) quietly voids the finding.\n\nA sample is a claim about who your evidence covers; recruit so that claim is true.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one recruitment decision from population to roster. A team is testing a tutoring app aimed at first-generation college students. The risk-driving trait is not age or major; it is unfamiliarity with academic-jargon navigation labels, which **cognitive load theory** predicts will cost these learners extraneous effort that experienced students never spend. So the screener targets that trait directly: it recruits students who have not previously used a campus tutoring portal, and it excludes peer tutors who know the vocabulary.\n\nNow apply **UDL learner variability**. Within first-generation students there is still a range: some are confident readers, some are multilingual and parse English jargon more slowly. A walkthrough plan covers both ends rather than filling all six slots with confident readers, because a label that confuses the slower reader is precisely the finding worth keeping. The roster, read back, is a sentence: \"Six first-generation students, none prior portal users, spanning confident to slower readers.\" That sentence is what makes any problem they hit credible for the real audience.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a recruitment plan, keep one column for who is included and one for who is excluded, and for each entry ask which problems that choice makes visible or invisible. The exclusions are usually more revealing than the inclusions; a plan that excludes the very learners who would struggle has engineered its own clean result.\n\nA strong reader of a sampling plan can answer four questions:\n\n1. What single trait most threatens this design, and does the screener recruit for it?\n2. Which subgroups must appear for the finding to transfer?\n3. Who, if recruited, would hide the problem rather than reveal it?\n4. How many participants per group, and what discovery logic justifies that number?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe sampling section is strong when a reviewer can read the screener alone and predict which learners will and will not appear, then agree those are the right people for the claim you intend to make. Treat the screener question as the artifact's load-bearing element; a vague criterion (\"some experience\") lets the wrong people through and reintroduces the contamination you were guarding against. Use the annotation tools to flag any slot justified by availability, and ask BigAL to compare your roster against the coverage check rather than to suggest more participants.\n\nA useful closing line reads: \"I changed the sample from five available colleagues to six warehouse staff screened by prior LMS use because the colleagues' expertise would have hidden the navigation problem; I rejected the suggestion to add two designers as a comparison group, since their data could not transfer to the real audience.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same sampling logic to a classroom field test where you cannot screen freely and must take the students enrolled. The discipline does not disappear; it shifts from recruitment to interpretation. You now document which subgroups your fixed roster happens to cover and which it misses, and you scope every claim to the segments actually present. A reader who treats \"the whole class participated\" as automatic representativeness has missed that an intact class is itself a convenience sample, with its own blind spots to name.\n\nWork the recruitment dilemma below. You are staffing a six-slot test of a first-generation-student tutoring portal, and every choice trades coverage against convenience.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Staffing a six-slot tutoring-portal test\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your risk-driving trait is unfamiliarity with academic-jargon navigation labels. Two peer tutors who know the portal volunteer. Do you take them to fill slots fast?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, they are knowledgeable and available\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Peer tutors already own the jargon, so they will never hit the label-confusion the test targets. They are maskers, not data.\"},{\"label\":\"No, exclude tutors and recruit first-gen non-users\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: you recruit for the trait that drives risk and exclude the participants whose insider knowledge would hide the problem.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The session runs smoothly and everyone finds every label. What did you actually learn?\",\"feedback\":\"Almost nothing transferable. A clean expert session is evidence the wrong people tried it, not that the design works. Re-screen for first-gen non-users.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Among first-gen non-users you can take confident readers or also include slower, multilingual readers. You have six slots. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Fill all six with confident readers for clean data\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"This clusters in the comfortable middle and erases the edge where jargon load is heaviest, the very finding worth keeping.\"},{\"label\":\"Span the range: some confident, some slower multilingual readers\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: spanning learner variability surfaces the label that confuses the slower reader, which is the credible, transferable finding.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"No label confused your confident readers. You report the portal is usable. Is the claim safe?\",\"feedback\":\"No. The claim covers only confident readers; you engineered a clean result by excluding the learners most at risk. The finding does not transfer to the real first-gen audience.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Read your roster back as one sentence.\",\"feedback\":\"Six first-generation students, none prior portal users, spanning confident to slower readers. That sentence makes any problem they hit credible for the real audience, which is what a defensible sample buys you.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/05-02-participant-profiles-and-sampling-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"05.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace for AIL 606 05.02. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your sampling plan names the risk-driving trait, screens for it, spans the relevant variability, and excludes participants who would mask the problem.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling\", \"ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling", "stem": "Your prototype trains warehouse staff with low LMS experience, but you recruit 5 instructional designers because they are available. What is the main threat to your usability evidence?", "options": ["The sample is too small for any conclusion.", "Expert designers will not encounter the friction the real low-experience users would, so usability problems stay hidden.", "Designers cannot give honest feedback about their own field.", "You should always recruit exactly 8 participants."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A convenience sample of experts is unrepresentative of the target population, so it hides the very problems the test should surface.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_convenience_equals_representative", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling", "stem": "Which screening criterion best targets the design risk in a tutoring prototype whose hardest moment is first-time onboarding?", "options": ["Recruit whoever responds first to the email.", "Recruit only people who already use the tutoring tool daily.", "Screen on prior experience with similar tools and include a band of first-time users who face the onboarding step.", "Screen on age alone."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Screen on the trait that drives the design risk; first-time users are the ones who will reveal onboarding friction.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_screen_on_wrong_trait", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "An AI tool generates synthetic participant personas and offers to 'run' the usability session against them. What is the defensible boundary?", "options": ["Replace real recruitment with the AI personas to save time.", "Use the personas only to draft your screener and profile categories, then test with real humans from the target population.", "Trust the AI session results because the personas are detailed.", "Avoid AI personas entirely because sampling must be manual."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Synthetic personas can scaffold a screener, but usability evidence requires observed behavior from real members of the population.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_synthetic_for_real_users", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_participant_profiles_and_sampling_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling", "stem": "A team recruits 6 frequent users of the platform, finds almost no problems, and concludes the design works for all learners. Why does this overclaim?", "options": ["Six participants is statistically significant, so the claim is fine.", "The sample covers only one slice of learner variability; results cannot generalize to first-time or occasional users who were never observed.", "They should have run an A/B test instead.", "Frequent users always find more problems, not fewer."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A sample that omits parts of the population's variability cannot support a claim about the whole population.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_one_slice_generalization", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_participant_profiles_and_sampling"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_convenience_equals_representative", "pattern": "convenience_sample_as_representative", "description": "Assuming an available, easy-to-reach group represents the target population", "trigger": "learner recruits colleagues or experts because they are available and treats them as the real audience", "feedback": "Ask who the design actually serves. A convenient sample of experts hides the friction your real, less-experienced users would hit.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_screen_on_wrong_trait", "pattern": "screening_on_irrelevant_trait", "description": "Screening participants on a trait unrelated to the design risk being tested", "trigger": "learner screens on availability, age, or general interest rather than the experience that drives the usability risk", "feedback": "Identify the design risk first, then screen on the trait that exposes it (for example, prior tool experience for an onboarding test).", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_synthetic_for_real_users", "pattern": "synthetic_personas_replace_users", "description": "Treating AI-generated personas or simulated sessions as a substitute for observing real users", "trigger": "learner reports usability evidence drawn from synthetic personas instead of real participants", "feedback": "Use personas to design the screener and profiles, but collect usability evidence from real members of the target population.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_5_2_one_slice_generalization", "pattern": "single_slice_generalization", "description": "Generalizing to the whole population from a sample that covers only one slice of learner variability", "trigger": "learner claims the design works for all learners after testing only frequent or only novice users", "feedback": "Bound the claim to the slice you observed, or recruit across the variability before claiming the design works for everyone.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}